1️⃣ What is IdentityHashMap?

IdentityHashMap is a special type of Map in Java where:

✅ Keys are compared using == (reference equality)
❌ NOT using .equals()

That is the BIG difference.

2️⃣ Normal HashMap vs IdentityHashMap
HashMap
Uses:
hashCode()
equals()
If two objects are logically equal → treated as same key

IdentityHashMap
Uses:
== (memory address comparison)
Even if objects are equal in value → treated as different keys if memory reference differs


6️⃣ Internal Working
Feature	IdentityHashMap
Key comparison	== (reference equality)
Allows null key	Yes
Performance	O(1) average
Order	No order guarantee
Thread-safe	No

7️⃣ When is IdentityHashMap used?
Very rare in normal applications.

Used in:
Object graph processing

Serialization
When identity (memory reference) matters
Framework-level internal operations
Not commonly asked in basic interviews, but good to know for advanced Java.


If interviewer asks:

👉 "What is IdentityHashMap?"
You say:
IdentityHashMap compares keys using reference equality (==) instead of equals(), unlike HashMap.